# Icon-only buttons must have a Tooltip

If a button (or button-like control) renders **only an icon** (no visible text label), it **must** be wrapped in a `Tooltip` from `@/components/ui/tooltip` so the action is discoverable on hover and focus. This applies to both pointer and keyboard users.

An `aria-label` and/or `sr-only` text is **necessary but not sufficient** — sighted users without a screen reader cannot read those. The tooltip is what makes the action visible.

## What counts as "icon-only"

- `<Button size="icon" | "icon-sm" | "icon-xs" | "icon-lg" />` with no visible text children.
- A native `<button>` whose only visual child is an icon (`<HugeiconsIcon>`, `<svg>`, a Lucide icon, etc.).
- A `DropdownMenuTrigger`, `PopoverTrigger`, `SelectTrigger`, etc. that renders an icon-only button.
- `SidebarTrigger` and any other icon-only primitive composed in product code.

## The canonical pattern

Use Base UI's `render` prop to merge the trigger onto the button. See `components/theme-toggle.tsx` for the reference implementation.

```tsx
import { Button } from "@/components/ui/button"
import { Tooltip, TooltipContent, TooltipTrigger } from "@/components/ui/tooltip"

<Tooltip>
  <TooltipTrigger
    render={
      <Button
        type="button"
        variant="ghost"
        size="icon"
        aria-label="Upload transactions"
        onClick={handleUpload}
      >
        <Upload className="size-4" aria-hidden />
      </Button>
    }
  />
  <TooltipContent side="bottom">Upload transactions</TooltipContent>
</Tooltip>
```

## When the button also opens a menu/popover/dialog

Nest the menu/popover trigger inside the tooltip trigger via `render`:

```tsx
<DropdownMenu>
  <Tooltip>
    <TooltipTrigger
      render={
        <DropdownMenuTrigger
          aria-label="More actions"
          render={<Button variant="ghost" size="icon" />}
        >
          <HugeiconsIcon icon={MoreVerticalCircle01Icon} strokeWidth={2} />
        </DropdownMenuTrigger>
      }
    />
    <TooltipContent side="left">More actions</TooltipContent>
  </Tooltip>
  <DropdownMenuContent>{/* … */}</DropdownMenuContent>
</DropdownMenu>
```

## Authoring rules

- **Always set `aria-label`** on the button with the same action verb phrase as the tooltip text.
- **Tooltip text is concise and action-oriented** ("Remove invoice", "Open menu", "Close comparison"). Avoid full sentences and trailing punctuation.
- **Match the user's perspective**: describe what the click does, not what the icon is. ("Delete saved analysis", not "Trash icon".)
- **Pick a sensible `side`**: default `"bottom"` for top-bar/header controls, `"left"` for trailing-cell actions, `"right"` for leading actions, `"top"` for footer pagination.
- **Do not** rely on `title` attributes — they are inconsistent across browsers and screen readers. Always use `Tooltip` + `TooltipContent`.
- **Do not** wrap buttons that already have a visible text label in a tooltip — tooltips are for icon-only buttons (or truncated labels).
- The app is already wrapped in `TooltipProvider` (see `app/layout.tsx`); do not add another provider.

## When in doubt

If you find yourself adding `aria-label` to a `<Button size="icon*">` or to a button whose only child is an icon, you **must** also wrap it in a `Tooltip` with matching `TooltipContent`.
